def quantumProcessingIdeas_statement75 : String := "NIST IR 8432, December 2023, defines genomic data as DNA sequences, variants and gene activity. Nothing is fetched here.\nidea: Fully homomorphic encryption computes on encrypted genomic data.\nidea: Secure multi-party computation analyses genomic data from several parties without revealing an input.\nidea: A zero-knowledge proof can validate a genomic result without revealing the data.\nidea: Federated multi-party homomorphic encryption is recorded for oncology and precision-medicine analysis.\n idea: NIST records that this is not a general solution, and cites Froelicher and others in Nature Communications 2021.\n  idea: In oncology it allows survival analysis, and in precision medicine it allows genome-wide association studies.\n   idea: It also allows machine learning training and testing, and the report asks for a demonstration project in the United States.\n    idea: The demonstration would test applicability and wider acceptance in the United States.\n     idea: It can speed oncology treatment and precision medicine by letting organizations collaborate without complex contract negotiations, because the risk of exfiltration or a privacy violation is virtually eliminated.\nidea: Differential privacy is named with those mechanisms as a privacy-enhancing technology.\n idea: It can be combined with federated learning or used to produce synthetic data.\n  idea: NIST records that there is not yet sufficient research to say the privacy guarantee holds once the set is combined with other data.\n   idea: Synthetic data from differential privacy add noise so that one person cannot be distinguished inside the set.\n    idea: The noisy set can be analyzed and shared.\n     idea: It also allows periodic updates as the data evolve, because the synthetic set can follow the underlying set.\nidea: Terra.bio and DNAnexus map their genomic controls to NIST SP 800-53.\n idea: A mapping to SP 800-53 does not replace the risk management of the organization.\nidea: NIH iDASH holds a secure genome analysis competition.\n idea: The competition studies cybersecurity mechanisms for genomic data systems."
def quantumProcessingIdeas_statement76 : String := "NIST IR 8467 is the Genomic Data Cybersecurity and Privacy Frameworks Community Profile. It prioritizes outcomes from the Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 and the Privacy Framework 1.0 across the genomic data lifecycle. Nothing is fetched here.\nidea: The profile is the first joint Cybersecurity Framework and Privacy Framework community profile from NIST."
def quantumProcessingIdeas_statement77 : String := "NIST CSWP 35, the initial public draft of 16 December 2024, is a threat model of the genomic sequencing and analysis workflow. Nothing is fetched here.\nidea: The draft is an example an organization adapts to its own sequencing workflow.\n idea: The public comment period on the draft is closed."
def quantumProcessingIdeas_statement78 : String := "The NCCoE project Cybersecurity and Privacy of Genomic Data collects NIST IR 8432, NIST IR 8467 and NIST CSWP 35. Nothing is fetched here."
def quantumProcessingIdeas_statement79 : String := "NIST records that genomic data is immutable, that health research depends on it, and that cybersecurity and privacy both apply once a sequencer has written the data. Nothing is fetched here.\nidea: A genome cannot be reissued the way a credential can."
def quantumProcessingIdeas_statement80 : String := "NIST ties the secure transfer of synthetic nucleic-acid sequence-screening data to the genomic cybersecurity profile, to supply-chain risk management, and to SP 800-63 identity proofing. Nothing is fetched here.\nidea: Sequence-screening data in transit is checked against the draft Genomic Cybersecurity Framework."
def quantumProcessingIdeas_statement81 : String := "FIPS 202 specifies SHA3-256 as KECCAK with capacity 512 and rate 1088, and SHA3-512 as KECCAK with capacity 1024 and rate 576, on the 1600-bit state. The OligoArchive paper hosted on CERN Indico writes a SHA-3 checksum into a DNA oligo. Nothing is fetched here.\nidea: SHAKE128 and SHAKE256 are the extendable-output functions of FIPS 202.\n idea: An extendable-output function is adapted in length, unlike SHA3-256 and SHA3-512.\n  idea: SHAKE128 has capacity 256 and rate 1344, and SHAKE256 has capacity 512 and rate 1088.\n   idea: SHAKE appends the domain suffix 1111, and SHA-3 appends 01.\n    idea: The sponge is KECCAK-p on 1600 bits for 24 rounds, then pad10*1.\n     idea: The padding appends a 1, then the smallest run of zeros, then a 1, so the length is a positive multiple of the rate."
